    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2027 Hyundai Bayon Finally Looks More Like a Box and Less Like an Egg\n2027 BMW F 450 RR Is the New Kid on the Block and I Didn’t See It Coming\n44 photos\nPhoto: BMW Motorrad\nMy wife’s cousin used to be a biker when he was in college. His life has started moving at a much slower pace when he became a dad eight years ago, though. We still talk about motorcycles when we meet, and I can tell he’s still itching to get back on two wheels soon.\nHe must have read my recent “Ridden” review , as he wrote to ask for some advice on buying a new bike. I wasn’t ready for what followed. “It’s not for me, it’s for my daughter.” I didn’t even know she wanted one, but apparently she’ll be going to the dirt track with her new electric ride on Saturday.\nStart’em young, right? I always say that step by step progression is crucial in this world. I’ve seen far too many young riders who were quick to jump on a 600 cc, supersport machine, only to end up in the hospital soon after the purchase. The European rule book didn’t stop them from claiming they had limited their rides, even though achieving the target figures was often times impossible.\nWhile that category of bikes seems to have faded away, many manufacturers have started paying more attention to the middle-weight sector, in between 600 to 800 ccs. But closing the gap from A1-compliant (11 KW tops/15 HP) to A2-compliant (35 KW/48 HP) machines asked for an even more category-specific product in the 400 to 500 cc region.\nIn the past, BMW has had the G 310 R in that category, and I raced one for a few weekends at the track back in 2020. The single-cylinder setup wasn’t something you’d expect from a sporty BMW motorcycle though, and it felt too far-removed from the S 1000 RR which is easily one of the most revered bikes money can buy today.\nWhen the German manufacture introduced the F 450 GS last year, I didn’t think about it too much. It wasn’t until I rode the company’s F 900 GSA earlier this year that I realized how much I might liked to have its “smaller” sibling when I was still a novice.\nSomehow I didn’t realize this model was part of a broader strategy, which is why I was almost shocked seeing the F 450 RR today. It looks like a baby S 1000 RR , which should be great for sales. And I’m sure it has more to offer than that. The 420 cc engine has a maximum output of 48 hp at 8,750 rpm, and 31.7 lb-ft (43 Nm) of torque at 6,750.\nEighty percent of the torque is available at 3,000 rpm, which means you don’t have to constantly rev this thing to the redline to enjoy riding it. The RR weighs just as much as the GS (178 kg), which means it’s just slightly heavier than the Aprilia RS 457 (6.6 lbs/3 kg). The 14-liter fuel tank should be good enough for a maximum range of 217 miles/350 km, and you get plenty of goodies for a package that could easily cost less than $10,000.\nI have mixed feelings about the front section of the bike right now, but will withhold any conclusions until I see it in the real world. There’s a lot of information to take in, and I’ll be sure to come up with a longer story analyzing more of the specs and comparing the F 450 RR to its competitors.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBMW BMW F 450 RR BMW motorcycles supersport motorcycles\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Gorgeous Aussie Tiny Home Is All About Warmth, Comfort, and Indoor-Outdoor Living",
